  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Alice and Bob play a game.\n\nThey start with the integer NNN and take turns, with Alice going first.\n\nOn their turn, a player must select an **odd prime factor** ppp of NNN, and subtract it from NNN (so NNN changes to N−pN - pN−p).\n\nThe player who cannot make a move loses (that is, if either N=0N = 0N=0 or NNN has no odd prime factors).\n\nIf Alice and Bob play optimally, who wins the game?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the value Alice and Bob start with.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the winner of the game — either `\"Alice\"` or `\"Bob\"` (without quotes).\n\n\nEach letter of the output may be printed in either uppercase or lowercase, i.e, the strings `Bob`, `BOB`, `bOb`, and `boB` will all be treated as equivalent.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1091 \\leq N \\leq 10^91≤N≤109"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2\n5",
        "output": "Bob\nAlice",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: 222 does not have any odd prime factor, so Alice cannot make a move and will lose immediately.\nTest case 222: N=5N = 5N=5. Alice can choose p=5p = 5p=5, an odd prime factor of NNN, and subtract it.\nThis makes N=0N = 0N=0, and Bob cannot move; making Alice the winner."
      }
    ]
  },
